The user is asking why `irq += val` is used in the SG2000 IRQ dispatch code. Let me explain this clearly.

Looking at the code in `sg2000_irq_dispatch.c`:

```c
void *riscv_dispatch_irq(uintptr_t vector, uintptr_t *regs)
{
  int irq = (vector >> RV_IRQ_MASK) | (vector & 0xf);

  if (RISCV_IRQ_EXT == irq)
    {
      uintptr_t val = getreg32(SG2000_PLIC_CLAIM);
      irq += val;   // <-- this line
    }


```

I'm looking at the dispatch logic that handles IRQs. The key insight is that when `irq` equals `RISCV_IRQ_EXT`, it means we have an external interrupt, but the PLIC doesn't tell us which specific device triggered it. By reading the `PLIC_CLAIM` register, we get the actual device ID, and adding it to the base `RISCV_IRQ_EXT` value gives us a unique IRQ number that NuttX can use to identify the specific interrupting device.
